# The project video, part one of two: a sprite sheet.
#
# Every frame is the real plugin rendering through the real FFGL sequence --
# this is the generator being *operated*, not a mock-up. Render with:
#
#   ./build/fbtest --sequence /tmp/fb-a --script tools/video.cues \
#                  --file docs/example-sheet.png --columns 5 --rows 4 \
#                  --size 1920x1080 --seconds 24 --fps 30
#
# Part two is tools/video-tiles.cues against docs/example-tiles.png, and the
# two are cut together by the backend's render.py. Two sheets and not one
# because the plugin's two cases pull in opposite directions -- a soft sprite on
# transparent that wants Fit: Native and the Alpha key, and a page of hard
# full-frame graphics that wants Fit: Stretch and no key at all. Either alone
# would be an honest demonstration of half the plugin.
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Continuous values are the 0..1 the host sees, not the physical quantity --
# see Controls.h for why there is no other kind. Counts and dropdowns hold
# their real value.
#
# The order of this part is the order of the argument: first that a page of
# stills is an animation at all, then that you can cut a run out of it, then
# what a field of copies does, and the stagger last, because that is the idea.

# ------------------------------------------------- 0s: a page of stills, played
# One sprite, its own proportions, on black. Nothing clever: this is the whole
# proposition, and if it does not read in the first two seconds nothing later
# will rescue it.
0.0   Background Alpha=1.0
0.0   Fit=0
0.0   Scale=0.62
0.0   Position X=0.5
0.0   Position Y=0.5
0.0   Rate=0.62
0.0   Mode=0
0.0   Copies=1
0.0   Key=0
0.0   Frame Count=0

# ------------------------------------------------------ 4s: cut out a run
# Frame Count from the whole sheet down to the first eight cells, so the loop
# tightens onto the ignition and never reaches the embers; then Start Frame
# walks that eight-frame window down the sheet, which is the same gesture that
# picks "run" out of a sheet whose rows are idle, walk and run.
4.0..7.0    Frame Count=0.0..8.0
8.0..9.5    Start Frame=0.0..6.0
10.5..12.0  Start Frame=6.0..0.0
10.5..12.0  Frame Count=8.0..0.0

# ----------------------------------------------------------- 12s: rate, mode
# Slower, then ping-pong -- the burst runs backwards through its own cooling,
# which is a thing the sheet cannot do on its own.
12.0..13.5  Rate=0.62..0.44
14.0        Mode=1
16.5..18.0  Rate=0.44..0.70

# --------------------------------------------------------------- 18s: copies
# One to nine. The sprite shrinks as the field grows, so the frame stays about
# as full as it was -- a count that quadruples the ink reads as a mistake.
18.0        Mode=0
18.0..20.0  Copies=1.0..9.0
18.0..20.0  Scale=0.62..0.30
18.0..20.0  Spread=0.6..0.52

# -------------------------------------------------------------- 20s: STAGGER
# The argument. Nine copies in step is wallpaper; nine copies a frame apart is
# one animation travelling through them. Nothing else moves while it comes in,
# because everything else in this video is a knob and this is the idea.
20.0..24.0  Stagger=0.0..0.26
